PART A -- explicit counterexample (brute force over all q^n vectors)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  q=4 n=3; C = <1+x>; |C| = 16, dim = 2
  phi = f(x) -> f(w*x), i.e. diag(1, w, w^2); Hamming isometry: True
  phi(C) is cyclic: True
  k=0:  dim hull_k(C) = 0   dim hull_k(phi C) = 1   DIFFERENT
  k=1:  dim hull_k(C) = 0   dim hull_k(phi C) = 0   same
  same weight enumerator: True

PART B -- group sizes (how much of M is a form-similarity?)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  q=4 n=3: |M| = n! (q-1)^n = 6 * 27 = 162
  k=0: #diagonals with a_i^(1+q^k) constant = 3; |Sim_0| = n! * 3 = 18 (index 9)
  k=1: #diagonals with a_i^(1+q^k) constant = 3; |Sim_1| = n! * 3 = 18 (index 9)

PART C -- exhaustive invariance check inside Sim_k
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  constacyclic codes enumerated: 12
  (code, similarity, k) triples checked: 432
  invariance failures: 0   OK as proved

  note: 48 diagonal choices are Hamming isometries but NOT
        similarities of the tested Galois forms -- these are exactly
        the maps that may (and in Part A, do) change the hull.

==============================================================================
VERDICT
  The GALOIS hull dimension is not a class function on isometry classes.
  Explicit witness: q=4, n=3, C=<1+x>, phi=diag(1,w,w^2); Euclidean hull
  0 -> 1, both codes cyclic with the same weight enumerator.
  CORRECTION (2026-10-20): the Hermitian (k=1) hull is 0 -> 0 here.  Over
  F_4 a^3 = 1 for every nonzero a, so every monomial map is a Hermitian
  similarity.  A Hermitian counterexample exists over F_9 (hull 2 -> 0);
  see final_check_A.py and FINAL_INDEPENDENT_MATHEMATICAL_CHECK.md.

  Therefore direction C1, as stated, is ILL-POSED and must be reformulated
  in terms of the similarity group Sim_k of the Galois form (where invariance
  holds, by the proposition verified in Part C).
